"money": 0.11}, "rating_margin": 14.25, "sigma_race": 5.2, "sigma_total": 5.62, "baseline_margin": 13.95, "analyst_adjustment": -0.15, "key_factors": ["Independent October polls show Hochul ahead by 11-18 points among likely voters", "Hochul is an incumbent in a state that leans Democratic by about eight points", "Prediction markets price Hochul's win odds at 95% or higher", "Only partisan Republican surveys show a single-digit race"], "rationale": "Nonpartisan polls in mid-to-late October consistently show Hochul leading Blakeman by double digits, with the newest surveys at 14 to 18 points, and prediction markets price her win odds at 95 percent or better. The only evidence of a close race comes from Republican internal polls claiming a four-point margin, which independent surveys do not corroborate.", "watch": "If independent polls move toward the single-digit margins claimed by Republican surveys, particularly amid Blakeman's attacks on energy costs and a commuter tax, the race could tighten.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["Incumbent Kathy Hochul (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Kathy Hochul, the Democratic incumbent, enters the final stretch of the New York governor's race with a clear lead over Republican Bruce Blakeman. Independent polls in October put her ahead by 11 to 18 points among likely voters, and averages of recent surveys place the margin near 12 to 13 points. Prediction markets give Hochul roughly a 95 percent chance of winning, and most race raters classify the contest as solidly or safely Democratic.\n\nBlakeman has campaigned on energy costs and a proposed commuter tax, and Republican internal polls describe a four-point race, but nonpartisan surveys have not shown that tightening. Hochul has also out-raised Blakeman and holds the advantages of incumbency in a state that leans Democratic by about eight points. The main question appears to be the size of her winning margin rather than the outcome itself.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_